     "body": "<p>I looked more into this, and I realized that creating an index for a single datastream in a way that does not index all datastreams is not a trivial task, given that the filesystem layout for any particular Fedora instance can vary:  it can be legacy or Akubra, and within Akubra, it can be split into any combination of n-tuples, with varying depths.  In short, in order to calculate the filesystem path for a datastream object in Fedora, we need to know what storage provider Fedora is using, and how it is configured – which means reading and interpreting the Fedora configuration itself.  To do this, we would need to introduce a dependency on the Fedora configurations, as well as duplicate path calculation algorithms implemented in the Fedora and Akubra codebase.</p>\n\n<p>This strikes me as an unacceptable approach.  I recommend closing this ticket as \"Won't fix\", and recommending that organizations with large repositories create the datastream index once, then re-use it.  </p>\n\n<p>Unfortunately, every time there is a change to their repository, their datastream index will need to be wiped and recreated; perhaps there might be a less onerous approach to updating an existing datastream index (but that's another ticket).</p>",
     "created": "20/Dec/19 5:17 PM",
     "id": "20940",
     "jsdPublic": true,
